  "abstract": "This submission attempts to improve upon the previous random initialization local search by substantially increasing the number of perturbation steps (from 2000 to 5000) and marginally increasing the perturbation strength (from 0.01 to 0.02). Starting from a random configuration of 26 circle centers, the robust `compute_max_radii` function determines radii and total sum. The aim is to allow more extensive exploration of the solution space and find a higher maximum sum of radii.",
  "submitted_tick": 313,
  "no_debugger": false,
  "cpu_only": false,
  "original_submission": {
    "content": "import numpy as np\n\ndef compute_max_radii(centers):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n    within a unit square, ensuring no overlap.\n    Includes a safety margin for robustness against floating-point errors.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    epsilon = 1e-9 # Used for general small value comparisons and boundary checks\n    safety_margin = 1e-7 # Ensures strict non-overlap for evaluator\n    radii = np.ones(n) # Initialize with large radii, will shrink\n    \n    # Limit by distance to square borders, ensuring a minimum radius\n    for i in range(n):\n        x, y = centers[i]\n        radii[i] = min(x - epsilon, y - epsilon, 1 - x - epsilon, 1 - y - epsilon)\n        radii[i] = max(radii[i], epsilon) # Ensure all radii are at least epsilon\n    \n    # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps.\n    # Using 50 iterations for sufficient convergence.\n    for _ in range(50): \n        for i in range(n):\n            for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n                rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                \n                # Check for overlap condition using epsilon\n                if dist_sq < rad_sum**2 - epsilon:\n                    dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq)\n                    \n                    # Handle cases where centers are very close or identical\n                    if dist < 2 * epsilon: \n                        radii[i] = epsilon \n                        radii[j] = epsilon\n                    else:\n                        # Target sum of radii is `dist - safety_margin` to guarantee no overlap\n                        target_rad_sum = dist - safety_margin\n                        \n                        # Only scale if current sum is greater than the target sum\n                        if rad_sum > target_rad_sum:\n                            scale_factor = target_rad_sum / rad_sum\n                            radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                            radii[j] *= scale_factor\n    \n    return np.maximum(radii, epsilon) # Final check to ensure all radii are positive\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Construct a packing of 26 circles in the unit square using an extended local search\n    optimization approach starting from a random configuration.\n    \n    Returns:\n        tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n            - centers: np.ndarray of shape (26, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n            - radii: np.ndarray of shape (26,) with radius of each circle\n    \"\"\"\n    n_circles = 26\n    \n    # Initialize centers randomly within the unit square [0, 1] x [0, 1]\n    initial_centers = np.random.uniform(0.0, 1.0, (n_circles, 2))\n    \n    # Calculate initial radii and score\n    best_centers = initial_centers\n    best_radii = compute_max_radii(best_centers)\n    best_score = np.sum(best_radii)\n    \n    print(f\"Initial score (from random config): {best_score}\")\n    \n    # Local search parameters\n    num_perturbation_steps = 5000 # Increased number of local search iterations\n    perturbation_strength = 0.02 # Slightly increased max displacement for a center\n    \n    for step in range(num_perturbation_steps):\n        current_centers = np.copy(best_centers)\n        \n        # Randomly select a circle to perturb\n        idx_to_perturb = np.random.randint(0, n_circles)\n        \n        # Apply a small random perturbation to the selected center\n        current_centers[idx_to_perturb] += np.random.uniform(-perturbation_strength, perturbation_strength, 2)\n        \n        # Ensure the perturbed center stays within the unit square [0, 1]\n        current_centers[idx_to_perturb] = np.clip(current_centers[idx_to_perturb], 0.0, 1.0)\n        \n        # Compute radii and score for the new configuration\n        current_radii = compute_max_radii(current_centers)\n        current_score = np.sum(current_radii)\n        \n        # If the new configuration is better, accept it\n        if current_score > best_score:\n            best_score = current_score\n            best_centers = current_centers\n            print(f\"Step {step+1}/{num_perturbation_steps}: New best score: {best_score}\") \n    \n    print(f\"Final best score after {num_perturbation_steps} steps: {best_score}\")\n\n    # Recalculate best_radii for the best_centers found, just to be sure it's consistent\n    best_radii = compute_max_radii(best_centers)\n    return best_centers, best_radii\n",
